<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Satraps</h1><p class="st-meta">20 BCE – 30 CE (approx.) · capital Mathura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Saka governors who ruled Mathura around the turn of the era and left the famous Mathura Lion Capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satraps</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> governors who took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, probably in the 1st century BCE, as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> power spread from the north-west. Their best-known ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula</a>, styled himself great satrap and issued coins copying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> designs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> ruled a city that was already a major religious centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura-lion-capital">Mathura Lion Capital</a>, inscribed in Kharoshthi, records gifts to the Buddhist community by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajuvula">Rajuvula's</a> chief queen. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sodasa">Sodasa</a> issued inscriptions in Brahmi, including one recording a temple to Vasudeva, which shows the rulers supporting both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> communities also flourished in the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Within a few decades the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, which became its southern capital and a major centre of sculpture. The satrapal system survived further south among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-satraps.jpg" alt="Northern Satraps" width="315" height="321"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indo-Scythian_ruler_Rajuvula.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>20 BCE – 30 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mathura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rajuvula, Sodasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Vaishnavism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit, Gandhari Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Satraps of Mathura, Mahakshatrapas of Mathura</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=5&amp;focus=northern-satraps" aria-label="Northern Satraps on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6720 1170 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Satraps lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7040 1426L6948 1391M7040 1426L6757 1168"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6948 1391h0M6757 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7040 1426h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7040 1426h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mathura. <a href="/atlas?year=5&amp;focus=northern-satraps">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

